Germany is in shock after an  anti-semitic attack on a synagogue in  the holiest day of the Jewish calendar  Yom Kippur the Day of Atonement a  heavily armed gunman tried to force his  way into the building in the Eastern  German city of Halle on Wednesday but  was prevented from entering he then shot  and killed a bystander in the street  before killing another person at a  nearby kebab shop well for more let's  bring in DW reporter Kate Brady who's  standing by for us in Halle and our  political correspondent Simon Young is  with us in the studio good day to you  both  Kate what are the latest developments in  this investigation I understand  authorities have been searching houses  there was overnight in the nearby area  of Landsberg there were some searches  done overnight and we heard from  eyewitnesses locals there in Landsberg  that that some officers were going from  house to house and also we've learned  since that at one particular apartment  was searched in isolating and as far as  we know the main suspect who was  arrested yesterday at 27 year old  suspect he actually came from isolating  but we're still waiting to hear from  authorities any more confirmation on  exactly what they found during those  searches and in what way they were  connected to the events here in Halle  and in Landsberg yesterday but the focus  here in hallo today as you can see is  this vigil which is about to take place  behind us here I'm currently stood in  front of the synagogue where that gunman  tried to enter yesterday during those  Yom Kippur services and as you can see  the crowd behind me is growing there's  people from Hallows jewish community and  also other locals from hala coming here  to pay their respects today both to the  two victims yesterday in that shooting  many of them bringing candles and  flowers laying them at the at the door  here just a few meters away from me as  well in respect as well to the Jewish  community here in hi which was attacked  yesterday by that gunman and just behind  this police van there is the door  through which he actually tried to enter  and you can actually see the the gunshot  so that the holes from the bullets where  he tried to enter through that door  yesterday but was fortunately unable to  enter  we're up to around 70 people were  actually attending services at the time  in the synagogue Simon you're following  this investigation as well of course  what more do we know about the attacker  and his motives well twenty  seven-year-old local man who neighbors  say was pretty unremarkable they hadn't  noticed anything about in certainly  nothing suspicious in this sense but  what police say they know about him was  that he had some kind of right-wing  ideological background and there are  some more concrete points because he he  published a kind of manifesto that he  put on the internet about a week ago in  which he said that he had the objective  to kill anti whites and particularly  Jews as he put it and of course this  incident took place on the Jewish holy  day of Yom Kippur and the man also  streamed he live streamed his attack  from a camera mounted on the helmet  online so you know in during that he was  making commentary expressing hatred  against Jews I would also say though  that he seems to have been more  indiscriminate than that when he  couldn't get into the synagogue he  killed a couple of other people who  don't appear to have had any specific  connection and I think this links to  another idea of you know these kind of  attackers is a video game style killing  fantasy of indiscriminate killing this  man also is alleged to have said that he  was happy to kill Muslims and  left-wingers so you know before we say  that it's a purely anti-semitic attack  we should also look at the whole context  and police are obviously investigating  that now well Israeli Prime Minister  Benjamin Netanyahu has expressed  condolences after yesterday's shooting  here's part of what he said on Twitter  he wrote the terrorist attack against  the Jewish community in Halle on Yom  Kippur is additional testimony that  anti-semitism in Europe is increasing I  send condolences to the families of the  victims and call on the German  authorities to continue taking  determined action against anti-semitism  Simon how serious  is the problem of anti-semitism in  Germany and what our German authorities  doing to address it well it is a serious  problem Jewish groups have been a  warning about growing anti-semitism for  some time and we saw anti-semitic  incidents are 20% for instance last year  with more than a thousand incidents  across Germany the 69 I think violent  attacks so it you know it is a serious  problem of what are the authorities  doing well showing solidarity with the  Jewish community is one thing they're  also talking about stepping up police  action for instance offering them the  opportunity to act more quickly to  identify anti-semitic crimes as a  specific class of crimes rather than  just general racist hatred also giving  courts the opportunity to put people in  jail for longer if they're convicted of  this kind of thing but also groups are  now calling for police protection to be  provided on a routine basis much more to  synagogues it's done in larger cities in  Germany but perhaps it'll have to be  done elsewhere as well now Simon thank  you very much DW political correspondent  Simon Young with me in the studio and  there in hala forest DW reporter Kate  Brady thank you very much to you both
 

